<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Counani</h1><p class="st-meta">1886 CE – 1891 CE · capital Counani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-proclaimed republic in the jungle territory disputed by France and Brazil, created by French adventurers and never recognised.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyapock-river">Oyapock</a> and Araguari rivers, now the Brazilian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amapa">Amapá</a>, was claimed by both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>. In 1886 a group of settlers at Counani, encouraged by French adventurers, proclaimed an independent republic, and the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-gros">Jules Gros</a>, who never visited it, was named president for life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic existed mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-gros">Gros</a> issued decrees, created an order of chivalry and sold titles and stamps. On the ground Counani was a small settlement of a few hundred people, mostly of African and indigenous descent, living from gathering and gold prospecting. Neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> nor <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> recognised it, and the French government disavowed it in 1887.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The republic faded by 1891. A gold rush in the territory in the 1890s raised tensions, and in 1900 Swiss arbitration awarded the disputed region to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>. Later attempts to revive the republic came to nothing.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-counani.jpg" alt="Republic of Counani" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Independent_Guyana_(1886-1887).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1886 CE – 1891 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Counani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jules Gros</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Independent Guiana, République de la Guyane indépendante</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1888&amp;focus=republic-of-counani" aria-label="Republic of Counani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3261 2071 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Counani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3581 2327h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3581 2327h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1888&amp;focus=republic-of-counani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

